   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_ITA_TRIE_13">via Sanità 2, <hi rend="superscript">III</hi>
                                </name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_ITA_TRIE">Trieste</name>
                            </addrLine> <addrLine>(Italy)</addrLine> </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: I enclose <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">an article about my writings</seg> by <name type="person" key="benc74">Mr Silvio Benco</name>, <name type="person" key="quin70">Mr Quinn</name>’s defence of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">
                            <hi rend="underline">Ulysses</hi>
                        </name> and a photograph of <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">the translator</seg> of <name type="work" key="W_EXILES">
                            <hi rend="underline">Exiles</hi>
                        </name>. The translation appears next week. About a fortnight ago, I think, I sent you some photographs. Possibly they also have gone astray. There was a railway strike in France then. Will you please let me know if you received them and also if <name type="person" key="poun85">Mr Pound</name> has sent on the <hi rend="underline">Nausikaa</hi> episode? I cannot find the <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">photograph taken in Paris</seg> but I saw it since I returned here so that it must be in the house. I shall look for it again.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>        	With kind regards</salute>
                        <salute>                    	sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>                                	<name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed> <dateline>
                            <date when="1920-03-10">10/iii/920</date>
                        </dateline>  </closer> </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary"> Silvio Benco, “Un illustre scrittore inglese a Trieste”, <name type="work">Umana</name> (Trieste) (6 July 1918): 1-3.</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">In her letter of 25 May 1920, Harriet Weaver thanked JJ for sending her the first act of <name type="work" key="W_EXILES">Esuli</name> (Firth 187). JJ must have sent her a photograph of Carlo Linati, the Italian translator of his play <name type="work" key="W_EXILES">Exiles</name>. Act I of <name type="work" key="W_EXILES">Esuli</name> appeared in <name type="work">Il Convegno</name> 1, no. 3 (April 1920): 28-52.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">In his <rs type="letter" key="L_68580">letter of 25 February 1920</rs>, JJ had promised to send Harriet Weaver a photograph taken in Paris in 1903.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
